WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.649
One of the props we see in the first scene of Lights Out
is a magnifying glass.

00:00:03.649 --> 00:00:06.820
This was before Things You Missed existed,
but it could be a little hint to the audience

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:10.900
that they might be rewarded if they take a
closer look at some of those smaller details.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:14.710
Details like this little figurine version
of the antagonist of the short film Lights

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:15.280
Out.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:19.580
The 2016 Lights Out is an adaptation of David
Sandberg’s 2013 short film created for the

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:23.240
Who’s There Short Film Challenge, at the
end of which, this happens.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.420
In fact, the opening of the feature film is
almost like a large-scale recreation of the

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:37.180
original short, using the same concept of
creating suspense while switching between

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:38.280
light and darkness.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:59.600
It also features a cameo from the star of
the short film, Lotta Losten, Sandberg’s

00:00:59.609 --> 00:01:01.989
wife who is also referenced in a deleted scene.

00:01:01.989 --> 00:01:05.750
She has appeared in all of Sandberg’s films,
some of which are teased at with other connections

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:06.750
in Lights Out.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.000
Stick around to the end of this video to hear
about them.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:14.880
Welcome to Things You Missed.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:18.070
After submitting his short film Lights Out
and uploading it to the Ponysmasher YouTube

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:22.350
channel in December 2013, David Sandberg was
awarded Best Director, and the video gained

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:26.830
significant traction on YouTube to the tune
of 9 million views by June 2015.

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:31.100
The Swedish couple was contacted by several
agents and producers including this guy, Lawrence

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:35.240
Grey, who wanted to turn it into a major motion
picture produced by James Wan, which probably

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.620
explains the abundance of mannequins.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:39.600
As we know, Wan loves his mannequins.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:44.570
I had the opportunity to see Lights Out early
at VidCon 2016, which was especially inspiring

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:48.280
because Ponysmasher had ridden the YouTube
wave to the Hollywood director’s chair,

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:49.880
which is exactly what I wanted to do.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:53.820
It was also one of the best movie watching
experiences because the audience was just so into

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:54.320
it.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:57.340
Surprisingly, considering it was a room full
of creators, I think I was the only

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:00.180
one who actually filmed anything from the
Q&amp;A.

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:03.580
“Can you tell us what it was like when that
call came in from Hollywood?”

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:10.320
“Well it was like 3 days after it went viral,
and I know the second day, a film festival

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:15.520
from Glasgow called us and asked us if they
could show Lights Out at a horror festival

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:16.900
there, and we said, ‘Wow!

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:17.400
Yeah.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.340
This is so cool.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:20.140
We need to go to Glasgow now!

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:22.280
This is our break!’

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:27.540
And then the day after that, Hollywood called,
so it’s like, ‘Well, maybe skip Glasgow

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:29.680
and go straight to Hollywood.’”

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:32.760
Let’s get into the Things You Missed.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:38.600
As Paul is Air Yeeted

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.180
into the darkness,
we transition into the title card, which uses

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:48.250
the very same logo created for the original
short, that now has lighting applied to it.

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:52.050
As the logo recedes into the dark, we see
two small glints of light, which represent

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.280
the glowing eyes of the antagonist, Diana.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:58.010
“One of the problems with shooting silhouettes
is you can’t tell which was the person’s

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:00.050
turned, or where the person’s looking.

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:04.630
For that, and just for the creep factor, I
wanted our creature to have eyes that reflected

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:05.120
light.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:05.780
Kind of like a cat.”

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:09.160
From the logo we go straight into the room
of our protagonist, Rebecca, and the first

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:13.180
thing we see is this poster, which kind of
represents a cartoon version of Diana.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:16.890
Another poster in her room is this band poster
for Slackt, which is a Swedish metal band,

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.580
and you may remember David and Lotta came
over from Sweden.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:21.170
There might also be a deeper meaning here.

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:25.220
On IMDb there’s this trivia point that says
Släckt is Swedish for extinguish, and to

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:27.900
extinguish the lights is basically to turn
the lights out.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.280
Later on we also see another Swedish metal
band poster for the band: Ghost.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:36.050
You could say that Diana is kind of like a
ghost in a way.

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:39.470
She certainly has ghost-like properties,
such as disappearing in the light.

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:43.040
There’s also an Avenged Sevenfold poster
in her apartment for their 2013 album Hail

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:44.040
to the King.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:47.230
Twenty-thirteen is when the Lights Out short
was released, but I can’t help but feel

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:50.290
it would have been better to put something
related to their 2001 song, Darkness Surrounding.

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:54.030
The next character we meet is Rebecca’s
half brother, Martin, who is seen wearing

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:58.110
a Batman shirt and also has a Robin figurine,
a couple of Justice League posters and even

00:03:58.110 --> 00:03:59.450
some Batman pajamas.

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:03.280
Now this could just be the studio, Warner
Bros, trying to promote their IP, but it also might

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:06.920
also be a sign that the director is a bit
of a DC comic fan himself.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:10.860
Afterall, he would go on to direct Shazam
as his third feature film in 2019.

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:14.220
There’s also sort of a connection to his
second movie, Annabelle Creation, which came

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.969
out in 2017, because the name of Martin’s
school is Palmeri Elementary.

00:04:17.969 --> 00:04:20.949
As you may know, I’ve already covered the
Annabelle films here on Things You Missed,

00:04:20.949 --> 00:04:24.540
and in the Annabelle Creation episode I mentioned
the easter egg: Palmeri’s Toy Shop, which

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:27.550
I connected to the first Annabelle film where
we see the Palmeri Apartments.

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:31.309
But the name Palmeri Elementary in Lights
Out doesn’t necessarily mean that Ponysmasher

00:04:31.309 --> 00:04:35.009
predicted the future, he actually ended up
clarifying in the comments that the name Palmeri

00:04:35.009 --> 00:04:38.331
is actually a reference to a woman named Victoria
Palmeri who works at New Line.

00:04:38.331 --> 00:04:42.639
I also want to thank David for going back
in time and adding the letters CZ to the eye

00:04:42.639 --> 00:04:43.639
chart right here.

00:04:43.639 --> 00:04:44.639
I made it!

00:04:44.639 --> 00:04:46.879
I finally made it in Hollywood!

00:04:46.879 --> 00:04:49.900
But speaking of connections with his other
films, there’s one major theme that has

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:52.840
come up in each of them so far, and that is
foster care.

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:56.169
In Lights Out, Martin’s parents are taken
out by Diana, forcing him to live with his

00:04:56.169 --> 00:04:57.169
half-sister.

00:04:57.169 --> 00:05:00.369
There’s even an alternate ending where she officially
becomes a foster parent.

00:05:00.369 --> 00:05:02.740
“You are now a foster parent.”

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:08.680
And if you’ve seen his other movies, they
both take place in foster homes.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:11.360
There are also similar ideas that Rebecca
has to deal with.

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:14.669
She has abandonment issues because of her
father abandoning her and her mother when

00:05:14.669 --> 00:05:15.669
she was a kid.

00:05:15.669 --> 00:05:18.569
That’s why she’s hesitant to make things
official with the guy she’s been seeing,

00:05:18.569 --> 00:05:19.060
Bret.

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:21.699
She’s afraid she’ll get attached and he’ll
also abandon her.

00:05:21.699 --> 00:05:25.150
She won’t let him stay over, leave anything
at her place or meet her mother.

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:29.159
Bret defies her expectations by staying with
her even after seeing how disturbing her family

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:29.820
life is.

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:33.279
Part of her character arc is her learning
to appreciate him as more than a friend with

00:05:33.279 --> 00:05:34.279
benefits.

00:05:34.279 --> 00:05:37.129
There’s even a moment when he goes to pick
up food for them, and while he’s gone, there’s

00:05:37.129 --> 00:05:38.159
a knock on the door.

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:41.360
In an alternate take found in the deleted
scenes, she simply says, it’s Bret.

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:45.840
“That would be Bret.”

00:05:45.849 --> 00:05:49.069
But the take used in the movie does a better
job of saying something about her character

00:05:49.069 --> 00:05:51.640
at that point, where she just sees him as
the guy bringing her food.

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:55.900
“OK. It’s the food.”

00:05:55.900 --> 00:06:00.100
But he’s actually kind of like the biggest champ in any movie probably, and even after

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:03.580
it appears that he’s running for it, he’s
actually going to get help from the police.

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:06.520
Maybe one of the most telling parts about
Bret’s character is how he supports Rebecca

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:10.540
even through her investigations of her mother’s
past with Diana at the mental hospital, where

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:12.920
we’ll find even more Things You Missed.

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:27.589
As I mentioned earlier in the video, Lights
Out was produced by genre veteran, James Wan.

00:06:27.589 --> 00:06:31.620
In music and video games, the producer is
always very creatively involved, but the involvement

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:33.340
of a producer in a movie can vary.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:37.400
However, it seems that Wan did actually offer
some creative ideas for Lights Out.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:42.440
“On the day we were shooting it, James Wan
was on the set and he came up with this idea

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:46.319
of having two Dianas in this one long unbroken
shot."

00:06:46.319 --> 00:06:49.569
I couldn’t help but notice that Rebecca’s
apartment shares a very big similarity to

00:06:49.569 --> 00:06:54.459
the motel James Wan’s 2007 movie, Dead Silence,
where a flashing sign from the street fills

00:06:54.459 --> 00:06:58.759
the area with a red light that goes on and
off, giving glimpses of the movie’s antagonist.

00:06:58.759 --> 00:07:03.270
Coincidence, homage, or idea from James Wan
himself, we may never know.

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:05.490
Unless David tells us in the comments.

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:09.229
Rebecca discovers a box of old files from
Mulberry Hill, the mental hospital where her

00:07:09.229 --> 00:07:10.749
Mom first met Diana.

00:07:10.749 --> 00:07:14.280
This was teased at earlier on in the opening,
when we saw Paul holding his wife’s file

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:18.240
from Mulberry Hill, describing the damage
that Diana presumably placed on her.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:21.389
There seem to be many examples of Diana attacking
the other patients.

00:07:21.389 --> 00:07:25.310
Rebecca listens to an audio tape recorded
by Sophie’s doctor, who is voiced by David

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:26.300
Sandberg.

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:30.960
“Patient 283, admitted October 6 1984.

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:35.620
The patient suffers from a unique skin disorder
that manifests itself in an extreme sensitivity

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:37.440
to light.”

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:40.969
There’s also an article that she discovers
about the light therapy treatment tested on

00:07:40.969 --> 00:07:44.710
Diana, and at the bottom, it’s cut off but
we can see that the chief of staff at Mulberry

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:47.489
is a Dr. Sand… something.

00:07:47.489 --> 00:07:49.949
I’m assuming that might say Sandberg.

00:07:49.949 --> 00:07:51.940
But I guess it could be Sandman.

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:55.720
Sandman was another DC Comics character, and
it looks like Rebecca has a couple volumes

00:07:55.720 --> 00:07:56.840
in her apartment.

00:07:56.840 --> 00:08:00.420
After attempting to stay over at Rebecca’s
house, child services puts Martin back with

00:08:00.430 --> 00:08:04.009
his Mother, and together they watch the 1958
film, Auntie Mame.

00:08:04.009 --> 00:08:08.629
Which I have not seen, but I looked it up,
and yeah... that’s what movie it is.

00:08:08.629 --> 00:08:11.540
But the interesting part is what line we hear
from that movie.

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:16.960
“Child, how can you see with all that light?

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:18.440
Ahh, now that's better."

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:21.840
Again, haven’t seen the movie, but it sounds
like she doesn’t like the light, much like

00:08:21.849 --> 00:08:23.389
Diana from this movie.

00:08:23.389 --> 00:08:27.149
This ties into the idea that Diana represents
Sophie’s depression and mental illness.

00:08:27.149 --> 00:08:29.979
You often hear someone who is depressed say
that they’re in a dark place.

00:08:29.979 --> 00:08:33.190
But I will cover that more when in Diana’s
episode of Horror History.

00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:36.519
They all prepare the house for the night to
keep themselves in the light and safe from

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:37.100
Diana.

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:40.520
One of the tactics they use is putting tape
over the lightswitch, which is another reference

00:08:40.529 --> 00:08:43.810
to the short film, and which is of course, also
on the poster.

00:08:43.810 --> 00:08:47.020
There would be one more reference to the short
later, when Rebecca and Martin are locked

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:50.550
in the basement and Martin goes through the
box of Halloween decorations to try to find

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:51.100
a light.

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:55.140
There’s a mask in there with the same white
glazey eyes as the monster in the short film.

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:58.690
Meanwhile, Bret is upstairs trying to save
them, but he gets attacked by Diana, and buys

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:02.770
himself some time by shining his phone light,
and we briefly see his phone background, which features

00:09:02.770 --> 00:09:04.180
the words “Scare Assault”.

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:07.880
Which seems like a band name or something,
but it’s not any band I’m familiar with.

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:09.970
He also seems to have a Scare Assault phone
case.

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:13.580
In any event, I think we can all consider
this movie a “Scare Assault” considering

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:15.311
all the scares in it,

00:09:17.960 --> 00:09:21.320
and there are some awesome
techniques used in the finale, like Bret using

00:09:21.329 --> 00:09:24.959
his car keys to shine the light on Diana,
and the muzzle flashes from the police guns

00:09:24.959 --> 00:09:26.329
making her disappear momentarily.

00:09:26.329 --> 00:09:29.750
That’s all the Things You Missed that actually
made it into the movie, but there are a couple

00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:33.529
things I noticed in the deleted scenes as
well, so I’ll just mention those quickly.

00:09:33.529 --> 00:09:35.860
In the same article that I mentioned that was written by Lotta,

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:38.459
it mentions a police officer named Eric Heisserer,

00:09:38.459 --> 00:09:40.310
which is the name of the screenwriter for
Lights Out.

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:44.279
There’s also a reference to art director
Shannon Kemp, who likely named the Kemp Apartments

00:09:44.279 --> 00:09:45.930
in reference to her own last name.

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:50.040
Diana is one of the best original horror movie
villains in recent years, so join me next

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:54.379
week as I analyse the entire history of Diana
Walter in the next episode of Horror History.

00:09:54.379 --> 00:09:59.040
Remember to subscribe to CZsWorld for new
horrors every week, ring that deathbell for

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:02.580
notifications and I’ll see you in the next
one.

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:04.760
Assuming we stay inside.

